Chapter: Builtins
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 A row's emitter is a `Maybe` because most names do not have one. 154 of
 the 265 carry a `Just`; the other 111 are effect ops, kernel syscalls and
 constructors reached by paths other than `emit-builtin`, and `None` is the
 honest answer for them rather than a stub that would route them here and
 then fail.

 `bs-alloc` IS "unknown" ON MOST OF THE 265 AND THAT IS NOT A PLACEHOLDER.
 It is the cost model's allocation class (`CostModel.md`), and the only values
 that may appear are ones somebody MEASURED: `none` for the text accessors and
 predicates (2026-08-15, 2026-08-19), the list readers and the two sized-vector
 readers, `fixed` for `__list-tail` at 24 bytes flat and for `vec-empty` at 16
 and `vec-singleton` at 24 (2026-08-25), `input` for the two list writers that
 grow with their argument and for `vec-cons`, which copies. `unknown` is read
 as allocating, so a wrong guess here
 would silently accept a `bounded none` that allocates, which is the one
 direction this check must never fail in.

 `budgeted` NAMES WHAT BOUNDS THE CALL, and may name an ARGUMENT (ruled
 2026-08-19, CurrentPlan 17 and 18). Bare `budgeted` is bounded by a constant
 the caller never states: `integer-to-text` cannot exceed the twenty digits an
 `Integer` has. `budgeted:N` is bounded by the Nth argument counting from the
 left, so `substring`'s row is `budgeted:3` because its cost follows the
 length it is asked for and not the text it reads from -- measured 16 bytes
 for a four-character slice of 64 characters and of 256, against 40 then 136
 when the slice grows with the source.

 The field is here rather than in a name list beside the checker so that the
 class sits with the name and the type, and so that adding a builtin puts the
 question in front of whoever adds it. `codex/test/cost/builtin-alloc` is the
 instrument that fills it in; a row moves off `unknown` when that harness, or
 one like it, has run against the name.

Section: The Table

 `list-push` and `list-snoc` MUTATE their list argument in place when the
 block has room, and return the possibly-moved pointer. That is the contract,
 not a defect, and it is why this compiler self-compiles inside its 2 GB
 memory budget: it took more than 4 GB before appends grew in place, and every
 attempt to give them value semantics has put it back. Measured: capacity
 equal to length killed `apps/brotli-hostile` and `apps/deflate-hostile`, and
 a build that copied at unproven call sites could not compile a three-line
 source at all.

 **A caller that needs the old list to survive an append copies it AT THE
 HOLDER**, where exactly one caller pays. Do not add a copying spelling, do not
 make the append persistent, and do not write a pass that decides between them.
 RULED by Damian 2026-09-03, and this note exists because the absent contract
 is what made the behaviour read as a bug and pulled agents into rebuilding it
 twice.

  BuiltinSpec = record {
   bs-name : Text,
   bs-alloc : Text,
   bs-varies : Boolean,
   bs-type : Maybe CodexType,
   bs-emit : Maybe (CodegenState, List IRExpr -> EmitResult)
  }

  ssend-name : Name = Name { value = "SSend" }
  srecv-name : Name = Name { value = "SRecv" }
  send-name : Name = Name { value = "SEnd" }
  schan-name : Name = Name { value = "SChan" }

  builtins : List BuiltinSpec
